          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.047,
            0.366,
            0.063
          ],
          "content": "Ganita Prakash | Grade 7"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.089,
            0.614,
            0.111
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Magic Squares in History and Culture"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.121,
            0.769,
            0.177
          ],
          "content": "The first magic square ever recorded, the Lo Shu Square, dates back over 2000 years to ancient China. The legend tells of a catastrophic flood on the Lo River, during which the gods sent"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.243,
            0.176,
            0.769,
            0.231
          ],
          "continues_prev": true,
          "content": "a turtle to save the people. The turtle carried a $3 \\times 3$ grid on its back, with the numbers 1 to 9 arranged in a magical pattern."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.788,
            0.088,
            0.929,
            0.228
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_11_image_4.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "bbox": [
            0.098,
            0.203,
            0.227,
            0.3
          ],
          "content": "| 2 | 7 | 6 |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| 9 | 5 | 1 |\n| 4 | 3 | 8 |",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_11_table_5.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.268,
            0.231,
            0.77,
            0.249
          ],
          "content": "Magic squares were studied in different parts of"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.244,
            0.249,
            0.846,
            0.286
          ],
          "content": "the world at different points of time including India, Japan, Central Asia, and Europe."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.245,
            0.286,
            0.847,
            0.323
          ],
          "content": "Indian mathematicians have worked extensively on magic squares, describing general methods of constructing them."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.322,
            0.848,
            0.396
          ],
          "content": "The work of Indian mathematicians was not just limited to 3 × 3 and 4 × 4 grids, which we considered above, but also extended to 5 × 5 and other larger square grids. We shall learn more about these in later grades."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.396,
            0.746,
            0.489
          ],
          "content": "The occurrence of magic squares is not limited to scholarly mathematical works. They are found in many places in India. The picture to the right is of a 3 × 3 magic square found on a pillar in a temple in Palani, Tamil Nadu. The temple dates back to the 8th century CE."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.488,
            0.744,
            0.526
          ],
          "content": "3 × 3 magic squares can also be found in homes and shops in India. The Navagraha Yantra is one such example shown below."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.764,
